1
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:14,200
I'm on a journey out to see the world.

2
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:17,160
While I'm on my own, I've
yet to reach the end.

3
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:19,860
So won't you come along with me right now?

4
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:23,777
Surely then, WE CAN!

5
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:26,780
Follow me now as we
journey on from sea to sea.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,220
For that treasure known
by everybody as one piece.

7
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:37,420
Even though they laugh and
say it's just a fantasy.

8
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:41,857
We are going, there's no doubt we go.

9
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:45,180
Cause we can feel the
rush of energy overcome.

10
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:48,340
It will lead the way.

11
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:53,500
Keep pretending that you know what to do.

12
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:57,397
Books will only get in the way.

13
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:00,000
No guides for today.

14
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:03,977
Do not worry, soon the rain
will be gone with the wind.

15
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:06,597
Keep your chin up, raise the
anchor, now that's what you'll do.

16
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:09,277
Give it time and calm the storm
that you have built within.

17
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:11,397
Come and have not forget number two.

18
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:14,757
Once tomorrow's wind picks up,
we set self cause we can't.

19
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:17,277
Thresh enough, stand tall and
take a look at one of you.

20
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:19,657
All the journey makes
me wanna see somebody.

21
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:22,617
There are things out there
you just won't believe.

22
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:25,760
Beyond your wildest dreams.

23
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:29,297
Don't think for a second.

24
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:30,757
It's not the truth.

25
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:34,840
That's the story most will believe.

26
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:37,420
Not me, come and see.

27
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:41,757
I am on a journey out to see.

28
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:43,317
The world done on my own.

29
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:45,337
I've got my group of friends.

30
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:47,377
Together we've got our way.

31
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:48,957
That's how it's done.

32
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:50,017
Says your captain.

33
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:51,277
Who says it can't be done.

34
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:52,937
We haven't even tried.

35
00:01:53,020 --> 00:01:55,937
Gotta just believe we'll
make it to the end.

36
00:01:56,020 --> 00:01:58,577
I just wanna do it all right now.

37
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:00,837
Together surely we can.

38
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:02,717
Yes, we can.

39
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:04,097
You can.

40
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:06,837
Just take this moment in.

41
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:08,057
We can.

42
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:09,437
I can.

43
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:12,197
Just take this feeling in.

44
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:13,477
Right now.

45
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:14,900
Right now.

46
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:18,700
Oh yes, we can.

47
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:26,277
Yes, we can!

48
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:27,190
Yes, we can!

49
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:32,940
It belonged to Shanks.

50
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:35,597
I bet you know about that guy, Cat Viper.

51
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:36,600
Very much so.

52
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:38,821
Goes by red-haired Shanks, right?

53
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:40,120
Too funny.

54
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:43,771
No wonder why that Straw Hat
was looking so familiar to me.

55
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:46,720
Oh, yeah?

56
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:48,850
Yeah?

57
00:02:48,933 --> 00:02:49,980
You said the same thing, too.

58
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:52,791
Were you pals with Shanks back in the day?

59
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:53,820
Indeed we were.

60
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:57,688
The Cat and I traveled alongside
he and Roger many years ago.

61
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:02,930
Huh?

62
00:03:03,013 --> 00:03:05,405
You were a part of the
Pirate King's crew, too?

63
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:06,800
Part of the crew?

64
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:09,906
I suppose that would be
one way of putting it.

65
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:14,472
We accompanied Lord Odin, serving as
his retainers everywhere that he went.

66
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:18,340
We even once sailed on
Whitebeard's ship, the Moby Dick.

67
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:25,220
So when Lord Odin was recruited by
Roger, we naturally escorted him as well.

68
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:26,990
Whoa, whoa, whoa, wait!

69
00:03:27,073 --> 00:03:28,016
Whitebeard, you say?

70
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:31,422
That's correct.

71
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:34,700
We were all rather young back then. So,

72
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:36,077
generationally speaking,

73
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:39,857
we were still apprentices around
the ages of shanks or buggy.

74
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:41,680
Okay, hold on a second.

75
00:03:42,140 --> 00:03:44,697
I'm having a little trouble
keeping up with all of this.

76
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:46,000
Now, let's see here.

77
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:47,937
We weren't always with him, though.

78
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:50,817
We didn't join those guys when
they made it to Laugh Tale.

79
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:52,780
I said slow it down!

80
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:58,260
Okay, so first there was
Whitebeard's ship, yeah?

81
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:01,940
And you said on it was Odin
and the dog and the cat.

82
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:06,440
Then Odin was recruited by Roger,
and he took you two with it.

83
00:04:06,620 --> 00:04:07,450
I miss anything?

84
00:04:07,533 --> 00:04:10,120
I remember it quite clearly.

85
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:12,420
We had so much fun.

86
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:14,016
Those were the days.

87
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:24,400
We encountered so many unknown
species, lands, and climates.

88
00:04:24,580 --> 00:04:26,640
A sea of clouds in the sky.

89
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:38,520
An underwater island where the sun shone
every day was an exciting new adventure.

90
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:43,520
We really shared some
good times, didn't we?

91
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:51,337
Hold up, aren't you two just
in each other's throats?

92
00:04:51,420 --> 00:04:55,037
And you did, you and the other somata—I
get to ride on whitebeard's ship too!

93
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:58,380
too Alas, we remained
at home back in Wano.

94
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:02,955
Truth be told, we did all we could
to block Lord Odin from leaving home.

95
00:05:03,060 --> 00:05:04,942
Unfortunately, that was in vain.

96
00:05:05,240 --> 00:05:06,070
So strictly,

97
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:10,597
in fact, that even stepping foot outside
its perimeter is considered a felony.

98
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:13,445
Lord Odin was somewhat
of a malcontent, though.

99
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:15,717
He was not shy about expressing his doubts

100
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:18,357
about the restrictions
segregating our home.

101
00:05:18,440 --> 00:05:21,260
Driven by this, he sought camaraderie.

102
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:25,802
With Whitebeard, Roger, and their
crews when they arrived in Wano.

103
00:05:26,160 --> 00:05:29,984
Honestly speaking here, they were
honorable men, far from wicked.

104
00:05:30,360 --> 00:05:33,889
Yet if we allowed him to sail
with them, there'd be outrage.

105
00:05:34,420 --> 00:05:35,920
So our duty was clear.

106
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:42,062
Well, anyway, that means you're
all buddies with Shanks then, huh?

107
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:43,220
And Rayleigh too?

108
00:05:44,180 --> 00:05:45,062
Hmm, certainly.

109
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:50,720
If you got to ride on Roger's ship, that
means it was the Oro Jackson then, right?

110
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,647
That was built by the shipwright
Tom, who I apprenticed under.

111
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:56,430
What?

112
00:05:56,513 --> 00:05:57,343
Incredible.

113
00:05:57,426 --> 00:05:59,260
Are you gonna really Tom's pupil?

114
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:03,057
If I may please interject, then
you must also know they're Dr.

115
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:03,970
Crow.

116
00:06:04,053 --> 00:06:04,883
One sec, Brooke.

117
00:06:04,966 --> 00:06:06,784
Sorry to cut you off like that.

118
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:09,017
I need to confirm something real quick.

119
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:09,930
Uh-huh.

120
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:20,358
Before we proceed, I have a concern.

121
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:24,386
You said the road poneglyphs will
lead us to Laugh Tale, right?

122
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:25,580
I'm a bit worried.

123
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:27,609
You sure about this route?

124
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:29,470
Look.

125
00:06:30,980 --> 00:06:32,451
See the log post needles?

126
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:33,900
Now, usually,

127
00:06:34,180 --> 00:06:35,957
to navigate to our next destination,

128
00:06:36,040 --> 00:06:39,452
we use the location that these
three are pointing towards.

129
00:06:39,640 --> 00:06:41,758
Shouldn't we continue to track them?

130
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:48,038
After Dressrosa, we followed Traffy.

131
00:06:48,420 --> 00:06:51,714
Turns out his Viva card was
what led us straight to Zoe.

132
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:54,520
Then we happened upon the road Poneglyph,

133
00:06:54,680 --> 00:06:57,445
which also coincidentally
points to Laugh Tale.

134
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:01,579
However, according to Crocus
back at the Twin Capes,

135
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:04,369
if you track the logs,
every single pathway

136
00:07:05,140 --> 00:07:07,277
will eventually converge into one trail.

137
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:10,772
I pretty much always assumed
that's where Laugh Tale would

138
00:07:14,820 --> 00:07:15,650
be located.

139
00:07:15,733 --> 00:07:17,520
Oh, you gotta know Crocus?

140
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:18,717
How's that guy doing?

141
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:20,859
It's been ages since I've seen him.

142
00:07:21,340 --> 00:07:23,137
He was good when we last saw him.

143
00:07:23,220 --> 00:07:25,455
That was around two years ago, though.

144
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:29,420
The old guy with the pet whale, right?

145
00:07:29,980 --> 00:07:31,600
He was our ship's doctor.

146
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:36,257
I remember that back in the day, he was
always on the search for some pirate crew.

147
00:07:36,340 --> 00:07:37,752
Oh, I bet that was mine.

148
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:39,900
Yeah, they're all dead now.

149
00:07:40,880 --> 00:07:42,740
Hey, enough with the chit-chat.

150
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:45,157
I'm trying to talk about
something serious here.

151
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:46,070
I see now.

152
00:07:46,153 --> 00:07:47,449
I understand your concern.

153
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:52,255
If you Karoo are curious to see where
the log post leads your crew next,

154
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:55,397
well, I do encourage following
it if you Karoo so choose.

155
00:07:55,480 --> 00:08:00,068
There is certainly a location in which
all three needles are pointing towards.

156
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:06,630
However,

157
00:08:06,713 --> 00:08:12,240
I know for a fact that your journey lies
beyond that position it's steering towards.

158
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:15,760
What do you mean?

159
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:22,800
Usually, when one reaches the log's end
point, they have a bit of a revelation.

160
00:08:23,340 --> 00:08:28,740
They arrive at a truth about the ancient
writing inscribed on the Poneglyph.

161
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:34,040
And the civilization that created them,

162
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:38,460
as well as the existence of
the unseen island, Laugh Tale.

163
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:48,940
When Roger reached that point, he
had to start his adventure anew.

164
00:08:52,420 --> 00:08:56,660
The crocus Yugara mentioned was
also part of the Pirate King's crew.

165
00:08:56,980 --> 00:08:58,804
He was a real Noodle type.

166
00:08:59,040 --> 00:09:03,060
He would not tell a lie unless
he wasn't fond of someone.

167
00:09:12,060 --> 00:09:13,120
I get it.

168
00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:19,497
So there really was no need for
me to worry in the first place.

169
00:09:19,580 --> 00:09:22,286
We weren't actually
falling off course at all.

170
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:25,980
Until now, I only had a piece
of a much larger puzzle.

171
00:09:27,200 --> 00:09:28,200
I'm so glad.

172
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:30,598
I didn't need to doubt the Log Pose.

173
00:09:30,740 --> 00:09:32,780
You've got a very sharp mind.

174
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:35,080
Fit for a navigator. Huh?

175
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:36,877
No need to fret.

176
00:09:36,960 --> 00:09:39,480
You got her on the correct path.

177
00:09:40,180 --> 00:09:42,080
Have faith in your own talents.

178
00:09:43,480 --> 00:09:44,740
Good job, Nami!

179
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:48,257
I didn't follow any of that junk,
but it sounds like you did, though.

180
00:09:48,340 --> 00:09:49,777
Good thing you're so smart.

181
00:09:49,860 --> 00:09:51,600
He said I had a sharp mind.

182
00:09:51,820 --> 00:09:53,837
I mean, obviously, but come on, guys.

183
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:54,750
Yeah!

184
00:09:54,833 --> 00:09:57,317
Three cheers to the awesome
navigator of the next pirate game!

185
00:09:57,400 --> 00:09:58,720
Nami is super cool!

186
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:02,537
Would you knock that off?

187
00:10:02,620 --> 00:10:03,450
That hurts!

188
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:11,180
It seems like all of you
got our wonderful friends.

189
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:21,997
Man, you guys have so many
connections to all these legends.

190
00:10:22,080 --> 00:10:25,727
It was hard trying to keep up,
just like when we met Rayleigh.

191
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:28,697
I also had the honor of
meeting Roger and his crew,

192
00:10:28,780 --> 00:10:30,477
but I was quite young at the time.

193
00:10:30,560 --> 00:10:31,900
So I hardly remember.

194
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:33,940
Isn't that a shame?

195
00:10:34,140 --> 00:10:35,000
Hey, hold up!

196
00:10:35,240 --> 00:10:37,620
He died over two decades ago, liar!

197
00:10:37,960 --> 00:10:39,300
You can't trick me!

198
00:10:41,400 --> 00:10:44,520
So much happened on our
adventures with Lord Odin.

199
00:10:45,220 --> 00:10:47,749
Remind me to go in-depth
about it sometime.

200
00:10:48,620 --> 00:10:54,260
For now, we have many loose ends to tie
up before we come face-to-face with Kaido.

201
00:10:54,600 --> 00:10:56,780
Indeed, we have much to prepare.

202
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:58,360
As I previously cited,

203
00:10:58,620 --> 00:10:59,817
in our home country of Wano,

204
00:10:59,900 --> 00:11:03,637
We are currently recruiting some
and I who will fight for our cause

205
00:11:03,720 --> 00:11:07,937
and in the meantime I would like to
get in touch with a certain someone.

206
00:11:08,020 --> 00:11:12,500
It never hurts to have even more
manpower at your disposal Who's that?

207
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:17,441
He's the former commander of the
Whitebeard Pirates first division.

208
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:26,520
Marco the Phoenix.

209
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:34,170
Marco?

210
00:11:34,340 --> 00:11:35,170
I see.

211
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:37,517
Though I never expected
his name to come up.

212
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:38,430
He's Marco.

213
00:11:38,513 --> 00:11:39,343
No crap.

214
00:11:39,426 --> 00:11:41,117
You guys are actually friends with him?

215
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:43,259
You think he'll actually come help?

216
00:11:43,740 --> 00:11:48,387
If he can lend a hand for the cause, I'd
feel so much better about our chances.

217
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:50,520
Hold up just a sec.

218
00:11:50,780 --> 00:11:53,817
After what happened with him
last year, he might be...

219
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:55,860
Indeed, it's a long shot.

220
00:11:56,220 --> 00:11:59,080
He may not agree, even if we locate him.

221
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:03,100
Marco, Marco, I know I
heard that name somewhere.

222
00:12:04,120 --> 00:12:06,417
Uh, maybe you met him back
in the War of the Best?

223
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:09,360
He was at Marineford, after all. Marco?

224
00:12:10,040 --> 00:12:12,580
Well, he has a very
rare Devil Fruit power.

225
00:12:12,780 --> 00:12:14,251
Does that ring any bells?

226
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:15,330
Huh?

227
00:12:15,413 --> 00:12:16,243
Rare power?

228
00:12:17,060 --> 00:12:18,589
He looks like a pineapple.

229
00:12:22,560 --> 00:12:23,420
Oh, yeah!

230
00:12:23,920 --> 00:12:26,600
He was involved in a
battle. What happened?

231
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:28,010
Well,

232
00:12:28,093 --> 00:12:30,737
there was a clash involving
the remnants of the Whitebeard

233
00:12:30,820 --> 00:12:34,140
Pirates versus the Blackbeard
Pirates about a year ago.

234
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:37,180
The world refers to it as the Grudge War.

235
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:41,596
But since the fight, the commander
and his folks have been disappeared.

236
00:12:42,160 --> 00:12:45,940
If Marco's still alive, I've got
a good idea where he might be.

237
00:12:46,140 --> 00:12:48,157
So, let's see if my hunch is right.

238
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:49,980
Whoa, whoa, whoa! Back up!

239
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:52,395
They fought with Blackbeard last year?

240
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:53,870
That's right.

241
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,357
It was a massive confrontation.

242
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:00,440
Both sides brought reinforcements.

243
00:13:10,960 --> 00:13:14,780
Marco, in the end, was defeated.

244
00:13:16,460 --> 00:13:20,931
Afterwards, Blackbeard received his
recognition as one of the Four Emperors.

245
00:13:21,080 --> 00:13:23,000
I had no clue.

246
00:13:23,620 --> 00:13:24,450
Blackbeard!

247
00:13:24,533 --> 00:13:25,363
That bastard!

248
00:13:25,446 --> 00:13:27,459
How did this war go over your head?

249
00:13:27,720 --> 00:13:30,837
Could you possibly be that
disconnected from the world?

250
00:13:30,920 --> 00:13:35,280
I do remember one thing Jinbei told
me after the war in Marineford.

251
00:13:35,740 --> 00:13:37,617
When I blacked out in that fight,

252
00:13:37,700 --> 00:13:41,380
some of Whitebeard's crew
stepped in and saved my life.

253
00:13:49,260 --> 00:13:52,397
Listen, we cannot give in to despair.

254
00:13:52,480 --> 00:13:56,237
